<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henrik Ibsen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henrik Ibsen (1828–1906) was a Norwegian playwright, often called the father of modern drama. After early verse dramas such as Brand and Peer Gynt, written in Italian exile, he turned to realistic prose plays that examined the morals of middle-class society, among them A Doll's House (1879), Ghosts, An Enemy of the People and Hedda Gabler. He is among the most performed dramatists in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen.jpg" alt="Henrik Ibsen" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eilif Peterssen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henrik_Ibsen_av_Eilif_Peterssen_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
